t about a Mixed Straight (8 points)? That requires chows in different suits. For example, W4-5-6, T4-5-?, B4-5-6. But I need three chows. Maybe if I can form a T4-5-6 chow by getting T6. \n\nLooking at the unshown tiles, T6 has 4 left. If I can get T6, then T4-5-6 is a chow. Then W4-5-6 and B4-5-6 would be three chows in different suits, plus a pair. That would be a Mixed Straight for 8 points. But I need to see if I can get T6. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can discard a tile that's not useful and wait for a specific tile. Let's check which tiles I can play. The valid actions include Play W4, W9, B5, B6, T4, T5, etc. \n\nIf I discard W9, maybe that's a terminal tile which is less likely to be needed for others. But I need to think about what's best for my hand. \n\nIf I keep W4, W5, W6 as a chow, that's good. Similarly, B4, B5, B6 is a chow. T4, T5, if I can get T6. So maybe I should hold onto T4 and T5 to complete that chow. \n\nBut I have to play a tile. Let's see. The valid actions include Play B5. If I play B5, then I lose that tile. But maybe I can form a chow in B with B4 and B6 if I have B5. Wait, I have B5. So B4, B5, B6 is a chow. If I discard B5, I can't form that chow. So maybe it's better not to discard B5. \n\nLooking at the unshown tiles, B5 has 4 left. So there are still some B5s available. If I discard B5, others might take it for a chow. But since I need it for my own chow, maybe it's better to keep it. \n\nWhat about B6? If I play B6, then I can't form B4-5-6. So that's not good. T5 is part of a possible T4-5-6 chow. If I play T5, then I can't form that chow. So maybe better to keep T5. \n\nLooking at the other options: Play W4. If I discard W4, I can't form W4-5-6. So that's bad. Similarly, W9 is a terminal tile, but I have only one. Maybe it's safe to discard. \n\nWait, my hand has J2. If I can form a pair of J2s, but I only have one. So that's not helpful. \n\nHmm. Maybe I should discard a tile that's not part of any potential chow. Let's see. My 